 ## Overview
-Hybrid Surface-Volume Rendering is a method for visualizing Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) data that combines physically-based atmospheric attenuation and illumination models for low-albedo gases with a hybrid surface-volume compositing pipeline [S1, unofficial]. Unlike traditional isosurface rendering which captures only a small fraction of the data, this system displays the entirety of the three-dimensional scalar data [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its sibling, Empirical Translucent Volume Shading, by employing a specific "gas-based" illumination model and a scanline-buffer algorithm that explicitly manages the interaction between surface-defined objects and volumetric gas sections via z-buffer fragments [S1, unofficial].
+Hybrid Surface-Volume Rendering is a method for visualizing Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) data that combines physically-based atmospheric attenuation and illumination models for low-albedo gases with surface-based rendering techniques [S1, unofficial]. Unlike traditional isosurface rendering which captures only a small fraction of data, this system displays the entirety of the three-dimensional data [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Empirical Translucent Volume Shading](4109-empirical-translucent-volume-shading.md) by explicitly integrating a fast scanline-buffer algorithm for surface objects with a dedicated gaseous volume rendering pass, aiming for photo-realistic images of transparent media [S1, unofficial].
 
